### exit=0
### --help
ch1m3ra 2.0 - packet sniffer built from scratch (no libpcap)

usage: ./ch1m3ra [options]
  -i <iface>   capture on <iface> (default: all interfaces)
  -l           list interfaces and exit
  -r <file>    read packets from a pcap file (no root needed)
  -w <file>    write captured packets to a pcap file (with -r: filter a pcap offline)
               .pcapng extension selects the pcapng container
  -W <MB>      with -w: rotate to file-NNNNN after <MB> megabytes
  --rotate-secs <n>  with -w: rotate every <n> seconds
  -H <host>    only packets matching this: IP substring, or a decoded
               DNS question name / TLS SNI / HTTP Host header
  -p <port>    only packets with this source or destination port
  -P <proto>   only this protocol: tcp udp icmp icmpv6 arp ip ipv6 dns tls http frag ...
  -n <count>   stop after <count> packets
  -s <len>     snapshot length, bytes captured per packet (default 65535)
  -B <len>     payload bytes to dump (default 96, 0 = none)
  -q           quiet: one line per packet, no details or payload
  -o <fmt>     output format: text (default), json (one object per line,
               jq-friendly), csv (header row + rows)
               extras: ndjson (json + "schema":1), zeek (TSV conn-style log)
  -e           timestamps relative to the first displayed packet
  -T <id>      follow a TCP stream: print reassembled payload as text and
               suppress packet lines (0 = follow every stream; ids shown
               as #N on packet lines and in the final stats)
  -F <expr>    kernel BPF filter (tcpdump-like subset): tcp udp icmp icmp6
               arp ip ip6, port N [src|dst] host ADDR, and/or/not/() with
               implicit and - e.g. -F 'tcp and port 443'.  Matching runs
               in the kernel (and in our own interpreter for -r); assumes
               untagged frames and no IPv6 extension headers
  -Z           zero-copy capture: kernel writes into an mmap PACKET_RX_RING
               instead of recvfrom copies (live capture only)
  -U           interactive TUI: colour-coded log, start/stop (s/x), scroll
               (j/k, PgUp/PgDn), search (/ then n/N), detail pane (Enter),
               pane switch (p), clear (c), follow (f), quit (q)
  -K           harvest cleartext credentials/tokens (HTTP basic/bearer/form,
               FTP, POP3, IMAP, SMTP, Telnet, SNMP, cookies, JWTs)
  -k <file>    like -K but also append each hit to <file>
  -O           passive OS fingerprint hosts (TTL + TCP options, p0f-style)
  -Y           top-talkers: rank hosts/conversations by bytes
  -N           learn hostnames from mDNS/LLMNR/NBNS/DNS responses
  -E           thorough filtering: parse frames instead of fixed BPF offsets
               (VLAN- and IPv6-extension-header aware)
  -A           reassemble IPv4/IPv6 fragments and decode the whole datagram
  -D           defensive anomaly detection: ARP/ND spoof, rogue DHCP, mDNS/
               LLMNR/WPAD poisoning, VLAN double-tag/DTP (passive, warns to stderr)
  --follow-out <file>  with -T: write the followed stream's reassembled
               payloads to a pcap you can reopen in Wireshark
  -M           do not enter promiscuous mode
  -C           disable colors
  --load-config <file>  apply saved settings first ("-" = ~/.ch1m3rarc)
  --save-config         write the current settings to ~/.ch1m3rarc and exit
  -h, --help   this help page

offensive / active tools (root + explicit flag; they transmit real packets):
  --arp-poison <victim>,<gateway>[,<iface>]
               ARP-spoof the pair to MITM their traffic (Ctrl-C to restore)
  --syn-scan <host>[:<ports>]
               half-open port scan; <ports> like 22,80,443 or 1-1024
  --dhcp-starve <iface>[:<count>]
               exhaust a DHCP server's pool with random-client DISCOVERs
  --replay <pcap>          re-send frames from a pcap (edit with --rewrite-src)
  --banner-grab <host>[:<ports>]  connect + read service banners
               (--banner-timeout <ms> read timeout, default 1500)
  --vlan-hop <iface>:<outer>:<inner>
               double-tagged 802.1Q frames to hop into another VLAN
  --bench <n>             decode the -r pcap n times and report packets/sec
  --deauth <bssid>[,<client>][,<count>]
               802.11 deauth frames over a monitor vif (authorised testing)
  --beacon-flood <ssid>[,<count>]  fake-AP beacon flood over a monitor vif
  --rewrite-src <ip>       replace source IP in replayed frames

examples:
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i wlan0 -P tcp -p 443
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i eth0 -w capture.pcap
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i eth0 -Z -F 'tcp and port 443'
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i wlan0 -U            (interactive TUI, start with s)
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i wlan0 -K -O -U      (harvest creds + fingerprint, in the TUI)
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i eth0 --arp-poison 192.168.1.5,192.168.1.1
  sudo ./ch1m3ra -i eth0 --syn-scan 10.0.0.1:22,80,443
  ./ch1m3ra -r capture.pcap -H 8.8.8.8 -B 256
  ./ch1m3ra -r capture.pcap -o json | jq -r 'select(.app=="dns") | .dns.qname'
  ./ch1m3ra -r capture.pcap -q        (note stream ids #N, then)
  ./ch1m3ra -r capture.pcap -T 3      ...follow stream 3 as text
### exit=0
